# WHAT THIS REPO DOES NOT KEEP FOR YOU.
#
# `Thurbeen/fleet` is PUBLIC. Everything a running fleet writes — your prompts,
# your briefs, your workers' results, your map of every repo you can reach — is
# working state that has no business in a public repository, so none of it is
# tracked. That is the first reason for every entry below.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated plainly because the README says it too: this repo does
# not back up your control plane's content. Your run logs, your project context
# and your map live in ONE working copy and die with it unless you back that
# copy up yourself. Nothing here is a substitute for that.
#
# Every entry says WHY it is here, because an entry nobody understands is one
# somebody eventually removes. Three reasons, and no others:
#
#   GENERATED   written by a script from a live source; reproducible
#   MACHINE     true on one machine — a path, a port, a pid
#   WORKING STATE  the operator's own content: what they asked for, what came
#                  back, and what they concluded

# MACHINE. Rendered from extension.toml.in by scripts/install-extension.sh and
# carrying this clone's absolute path, which is true only on one machine —
# extension.toml.in's header says why the path cannot be a `~`.
/extension.toml

# WORKING STATE. Your GitHub owners. registry/owners.example.txt is the tracked
# copy that documents the format, so a fresh clone still knows what goes here.
/registry/owners.txt

# GENERATED. Rebuilt wholesale from your `gh` session by scripts/sync-registry.sh,
# and an index of every repo you can reach is not a thing to publish.
/registry/repos.generated.yaml

# WORKING STATE. The judgement about one of your projects, written from
# _TEMPLATE.md. The form is tracked; what you write into it is yours.
/registry/context/*.md
!/registry/context/_TEMPLATE.md

# WORKING STATE. One file per orchestration run — the record of what you did,
# when, and how it went. _TEMPLATE.md is the form and stays.
/orchestration/runs/*.md
!/orchestration/runs/_TEMPLATE.md

# WORKING STATE. The task queue: topics, per-task briefs, the transitions a run
# produced, and what each worker concluded. This is the whole of the control
# plane's working memory — one operator's prompts and one operator's work — and
# it is exactly what must not reach a public repo. Two tracked files stay:
# README.md, which documents the layout, and POLICY.md, the standing policy
# every brief points its worker at instead of restating it.
/orchestration/queue/*
!/orchestration/queue/README.md
!/orchestration/queue/POLICY.md

# GENERATED. Python's bytecode cache. scripts/lib/webui.py loads queue.py as a
# module, and importing anything writes one of these beside the source — so it
# appears the first time the monitor is started and never in a fresh clone.
__pycache__/

# MACHINE + WORKING STATE. The monitor's runtime state: the port it chose at
# bind time, its supervisor's pid, its log, and the `down` flag that keeps an
# explicit stop honoured across a restart, a reboot and the next onboarding
# run. The server's CODE is tracked (scripts/webui.sh, scripts/lib/webui.py);
# everything it writes at runtime is not.
/orchestration/webui/
